WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.968 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:01.968 --> 00:00:03.936 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:03.936 --> 00:00:06.396 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:06.396 --> 00:00:22.277 align:middle line:90%


00:00:22.277 --> 00:00:23.800 align:middle line:84%
SPEAKER 2: Before
proceeding, please

00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:25.570 align:middle line:90%
view the following video--

00:00:25.570 --> 00:00:30.212 align:middle line:84%
Mashable, "Google's AI Assistant
Can Now Make Real Phone Calls."

00:00:30.212 --> 00:00:31.670 align:middle line:84%
BRANDON LESHCHINSKIY:
Hi, everyone.

00:00:31.670 --> 00:00:32.560 align:middle line:90%
My name is Brandon Leshchinskiy.

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:34.130 align:middle line:90%
Thank you so much for having me.

00:00:34.130 --> 00:00:37.270 align:middle line:84%
What we just saw was a
very good example of,

00:00:37.270 --> 00:00:41.170 align:middle line:84%
I think, what most
people would call AI.

00:00:41.170 --> 00:00:45.430 align:middle line:90%
Now, let me ask, what is AI?

00:00:45.430 --> 00:00:47.573 align:middle line:84%
SPEAKER 1: Answers
from participants:

00:00:47.573 --> 00:00:48.990 align:middle line:84%
SPEAKER 2: Artificial
intelligence

00:00:48.990 --> 00:00:50.790 align:middle line:90%
is something that can learn.

00:00:50.790 --> 00:00:53.130 align:middle line:84%
It's more than a program
that follows rules.

00:00:53.130 --> 00:00:56.170 align:middle line:90%
It's able to adapt.

00:00:56.170 --> 00:00:59.435 align:middle line:84%
SPEAKER 1: Something to
make our lives easier.

00:00:59.435 --> 00:01:02.090 align:middle line:84%
SPEAKER 2: A machine that
simulates human decision-making

00:01:02.090 --> 00:01:03.890 align:middle line:84%
and understanding to
a degree that it's

00:01:03.890 --> 00:01:07.125 align:middle line:84%
indistinguishable
from a human being.

00:01:07.125 --> 00:01:08.750 align:middle line:84%
BRANDON LESHCHINSKIY:
So already, we're

00:01:08.750 --> 00:01:10.610 align:middle line:84%
seeing one of the
issues here, is

00:01:10.610 --> 00:01:12.170 align:middle line:84%
that "AI" is a very
overloaded term.

00:01:12.170 --> 00:01:15.020 align:middle line:84%
I think most typical
definitions are

00:01:15.020 --> 00:01:19.460 align:middle line:84%
closest to that last one, that
artificial intelligence is when

00:01:19.460 --> 00:01:23.480 align:middle line:84%
a machine or a
computer can exhibit

00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:28.040 align:middle line:84%
some signs of
human-like intelligence.

00:01:28.040 --> 00:01:29.210 align:middle line:90%
Somebody mentioned learning.

00:01:29.210 --> 00:01:31.543 align:middle line:84%
That would be a subset of
artificial intelligence, which

00:01:31.543 --> 00:01:33.090 align:middle line:90%
we'll get into.

00:01:33.090 --> 00:01:36.760 align:middle line:84%
But in any case,
part of the point

00:01:36.760 --> 00:01:39.130 align:middle line:84%
here is that AI is a
very overloaded term that

00:01:39.130 --> 00:01:39.940 align:middle line:84%
can mean a lot of
different things

00:01:39.940 --> 00:01:41.148 align:middle line:90%
to a lot of different people.

00:01:41.148 --> 00:01:42.970 align:middle line:84%
And the joke in
the AI community is

00:01:42.970 --> 00:01:45.437 align:middle line:84%
that it's AI until we
figure out how to do it,

00:01:45.437 --> 00:01:47.020 align:middle line:84%
and then after that,
it's just coding.

00:01:47.020 --> 00:01:50.185 align:middle line:90%


00:01:50.185 --> 00:01:52.060 align:middle line:84%
Like I said, thank you
so much for having me.

00:01:52.060 --> 00:01:52.852 align:middle line:90%
My name is Brandon.

00:01:52.852 --> 00:01:54.240 align:middle line:90%
I'm a recent graduate of MIT.

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:56.775 align:middle line:84%
And in today's workshop,
we'll cover the basics

00:01:56.775 --> 00:01:57.900 align:middle line:90%
of artificial intelligence.

00:01:57.900 --> 00:01:59.317 align:middle line:84%
We'll talk about
machine learning.

00:01:59.317 --> 00:02:00.870 align:middle line:84%
We'll train an
algorithm together.

00:02:00.870 --> 00:02:02.760 align:middle line:84%
And I'll leave you with
a couple of questions

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:04.320 align:middle line:90%
and a word of caution.

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:06.180 align:middle line:84%
Before we get into
all of that, I

00:02:06.180 --> 00:02:08.400 align:middle line:84%
want to start with the
main takeaway, which

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:11.160 align:middle line:84%
is, with the right data
and the right model,

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:13.890 align:middle line:84%
artificial intelligence
can solve many problems.

00:02:13.890 --> 00:02:17.850 align:middle line:84%
But choosing the right problem,
finding the right data,

00:02:17.850 --> 00:02:20.530 align:middle line:84%
and training the right
model can be difficult.

00:02:20.530 --> 00:02:21.990 align:middle line:84%
So if there's
nothing else that you

00:02:21.990 --> 00:02:27.820 align:middle line:84%
remember from today's workshop,
this slide is the key takeaway.

00:02:27.820 --> 00:02:30.310 align:middle line:84%
And like I said, we'll cover
artificial intelligence.

00:02:30.310 --> 00:02:32.620 align:middle line:84%
We will see a few
different examples

00:02:32.620 --> 00:02:34.507 align:middle line:84%
of tools that can
be used to implement

00:02:34.507 --> 00:02:36.340 align:middle line:84%
artificial intelligence,
including something

00:02:36.340 --> 00:02:37.383 align:middle line:90%
called machine learning.

00:02:37.383 --> 00:02:39.550 align:middle line:84%
We'll talk about different
kinds of machine learning

00:02:39.550 --> 00:02:42.400 align:middle line:84%
and different problems that can
be solved by machine learning.

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:44.680 align:middle line:84%
And let me just say, since,
probably, a lot of you

00:02:44.680 --> 00:02:46.850 align:middle line:84%
are already wondering,
what is deep learning?

00:02:46.850 --> 00:02:50.230 align:middle line:84%
Deep learning is either a
subset or a subset of a subset

00:02:50.230 --> 00:02:51.980 align:middle line:90%
of machine learning.

00:02:51.980 --> 00:02:54.730 align:middle line:84%
So for those of you who are
familiar with neural networks

00:02:54.730 --> 00:02:57.340 align:middle line:84%
or deep learning,
that is a tool that

00:02:57.340 --> 00:02:59.140 align:middle line:84%
might be used in machine
learning problems.

00:02:59.140 --> 00:03:01.428 align:middle line:84%
We won't talk about that
in any depth at all today,

00:03:01.428 --> 00:03:03.220 align:middle line:84%
but I hope that by the
end of the workshop,

00:03:03.220 --> 00:03:06.460 align:middle line:84%
you guys will have the
fundamentals necessary to dig

00:03:06.460 --> 00:03:09.280 align:middle line:84%
into deep learning
at a conceptual level

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:13.540 align:middle line:84%
if you want to do
that some other time.

00:03:13.540 --> 00:03:17.110 align:middle line:84%
All that being said, let's
get into basics of AI.

00:03:17.110 --> 00:03:21.720 align:middle line:84%
So AI can be general
or it can be narrow.

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:23.910 align:middle line:84%
When we talk about general
artificial intelligence,

00:03:23.910 --> 00:03:25.800 align:middle line:84%
it's when a machine
can learn any task

00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:26.970 align:middle line:90%
that a human can perform.

00:03:26.970 --> 00:03:28.710 align:middle line:90%
This does not exist.

00:03:28.710 --> 00:03:30.870 align:middle line:90%
There are no Terminator robots.

00:03:30.870 --> 00:03:34.710 align:middle line:84%
There are no-- there is no
such thing as general AI.

00:03:34.710 --> 00:03:38.010 align:middle line:84%
And experts vary widely in
terms of their predictions

00:03:38.010 --> 00:03:40.450 align:middle line:84%
on when this may
actually happen.

00:03:40.450 --> 00:03:44.165 align:middle line:84%
So there are some people
who say, oh, 10, 20 years,

00:03:44.165 --> 00:03:46.290 align:middle line:84%
there are some people who
say not in our lifetimes,

00:03:46.290 --> 00:03:48.240 align:middle line:84%
and some people
who say, not ever.

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:51.360 align:middle line:84%
So really, really
broad range of guesses.

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:54.820 align:middle line:90%
In any case, most AI--

00:03:54.820 --> 00:03:58.000 align:middle line:84%
sorry, all of the AI that
exists today and most of the AI

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:01.750 align:middle line:84%
that you hear people
talking about is narrow.

00:04:01.750 --> 00:04:04.420 align:middle line:84%
And narrow AI is when a
computer exhibits intelligence

00:04:04.420 --> 00:04:06.950 align:middle line:90%
at one task.

00:04:06.950 --> 00:04:08.950 align:middle line:84%
To be clear, you can do
pretty impressive things

00:04:08.950 --> 00:04:10.360 align:middle line:90%
with narrow AI.

00:04:10.360 --> 00:04:14.410 align:middle line:84%
A self-driving car for example,
has one narrow AI system

00:04:14.410 --> 00:04:16.018 align:middle line:90%
that does vision.

00:04:16.018 --> 00:04:17.810 align:middle line:84%
It looks at the road
and interprets things.

00:04:17.810 --> 00:04:19.899 align:middle line:84%
There's another AI system
that does steering,

00:04:19.899 --> 00:04:21.003 align:middle line:90%
that controls the car.

00:04:21.003 --> 00:04:23.170 align:middle line:84%
There's another AI system
that does route planning--

00:04:23.170 --> 00:04:25.540 align:middle line:84%
that says, on a broad
level, where are we

00:04:25.540 --> 00:04:26.920 align:middle line:90%
going, how do we get there?

00:04:26.920 --> 00:04:28.888 align:middle line:84%
And when you stitch
all of those together,

00:04:28.888 --> 00:04:30.930 align:middle line:84%
you can actually create
a very impressive system.

00:04:30.930 --> 00:04:34.230 align:middle line:84%
But you cannot sit this car
down and play chess with it.

00:04:34.230 --> 00:04:36.390 align:middle line:84%
This car will not
learn how to paint.

00:04:36.390 --> 00:04:40.970 align:middle line:84%
This car will not learn
how to speak Russian.

00:04:40.970 --> 00:04:44.690 align:middle line:84%
It's doing a specific set
of tasks, each of which

00:04:44.690 --> 00:04:46.440 align:middle line:90%
has been trained separately.

00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:48.680 align:middle line:90%
And that is narrow AI.

00:04:48.680 --> 00:04:50.780 align:middle line:84%
So from now on,
when I say AI, I'm

00:04:50.780 --> 00:04:53.660 align:middle line:84%
talking about narrow
artificial intelligence.

00:04:53.660 --> 00:04:55.775 align:middle line:90%
Any questions so far?

00:04:55.775 --> 00:04:58.545 align:middle line:84%
SPEAKER 2: Question
from participant:

00:04:58.545 --> 00:05:00.170 align:middle line:84%
SPEAKER 1: What
category do you fall in

00:05:00.170 --> 00:05:01.670 align:middle line:90%
with regard to general AI?

00:05:01.670 --> 00:05:03.425 align:middle line:84%
When do you think
it will be possible?

00:05:03.425 --> 00:05:04.773 align:middle line:90%


00:05:04.773 --> 00:05:06.440 align:middle line:84%
BRANDON LESHCHINSKIY:
I don't think it's

00:05:06.440 --> 00:05:07.910 align:middle line:90%
going to happen for a while--

00:05:07.910 --> 00:05:09.200 align:middle line:90%
like many, many decades.

00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:11.730 align:middle line:84%
But I also think
that it's definitely

00:05:11.730 --> 00:05:12.980 align:middle line:90%
going to happen at some point.

00:05:12.980 --> 00:05:15.710 align:middle line:90%


00:05:15.710 --> 00:05:18.950 align:middle line:90%
Many decades to maybe centuries.

00:05:18.950 --> 00:05:19.550 align:middle line:90%
I don't know.

00:05:19.550 --> 00:05:22.500 align:middle line:84%
I Think technological
progress is hard to guess at.

00:05:22.500 --> 00:05:25.130 align:middle line:90%
So I think it's a fool's errand.

00:05:25.130 --> 00:05:29.220 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, so what does
narrow AI actually do?

00:05:29.220 --> 00:05:31.940 align:middle line:84%
Well, typical tasks, as we've
seen with the self-driving car

00:05:31.940 --> 00:05:34.790 align:middle line:84%
example, include vision,
language processing,

00:05:34.790 --> 00:05:35.960 align:middle line:90%
and planning.

00:05:35.960 --> 00:05:37.500 align:middle line:84%
These are just a
couple of examples.

00:05:37.500 --> 00:05:40.700 align:middle line:84%
So vision is going to be
understanding or creating

00:05:40.700 --> 00:05:42.080 align:middle line:90%
images or video.

00:05:42.080 --> 00:05:44.450 align:middle line:84%
Language processing--
understanding or creating

00:05:44.450 --> 00:05:45.590 align:middle line:90%
speech or text.

00:05:45.590 --> 00:05:48.020 align:middle line:84%
That's going to be, like,
Siri or Amazon Alexa.

00:05:48.020 --> 00:05:49.400 align:middle line:90%
And planning.

00:05:49.400 --> 00:05:50.900 align:middle line:84%
Lots of different
kinds of planning.

00:05:50.900 --> 00:05:55.310 align:middle line:84%
You can do route planning,
motion planning, task planning.

00:05:55.310 --> 00:05:59.090 align:middle line:84%
All of these things fall under
the category of narrow AI tasks

00:05:59.090 --> 00:06:01.810 align:middle line:84%
that you can train
an algorithm to do.

00:06:01.810 --> 00:06:04.030 align:middle line:84%
As far as how we
actually build AI,

00:06:04.030 --> 00:06:06.280 align:middle line:90%
there's a lot of different ways.

00:06:06.280 --> 00:06:10.237 align:middle line:84%
Two classical ways to implement
artificial intelligence--

00:06:10.237 --> 00:06:12.320 align:middle line:84%
and I remember learning
about this and being like,

00:06:12.320 --> 00:06:13.862 align:middle line:84%
wow, this is not
intelligence at all.

00:06:13.862 --> 00:06:14.890 align:middle line:90%
This is super dumb.

00:06:14.890 --> 00:06:17.150 align:middle line:84%
Are expert systems
and tree search.

00:06:17.150 --> 00:06:19.510 align:middle line:84%
So in an expert system,
it's just a person

00:06:19.510 --> 00:06:20.620 align:middle line:90%
writing a list of rules.

00:06:20.620 --> 00:06:21.670 align:middle line:90%
If this, then that.

00:06:21.670 --> 00:06:24.200 align:middle line:90%
If this, then that.

00:06:24.200 --> 00:06:29.600 align:middle line:84%
This can be super useful
for certain problems.

00:06:29.600 --> 00:06:31.820 align:middle line:84%
But a lot of the
things that we actually

00:06:31.820 --> 00:06:34.040 align:middle line:84%
think of as really
straightforward turn out

00:06:34.040 --> 00:06:39.630 align:middle line:84%
to be quite difficult.
So for example,

00:06:39.630 --> 00:06:42.230 align:middle line:84%
we can identify a
table like that--

00:06:42.230 --> 00:06:43.320 align:middle line:90%
very, very easily.

00:06:43.320 --> 00:06:45.080 align:middle line:90%
We know what the table is.

00:06:45.080 --> 00:06:48.140 align:middle line:84%
But I challenge you to
write a list of rules

00:06:48.140 --> 00:06:50.090 align:middle line:90%
that can define a table.

00:06:50.090 --> 00:06:50.840 align:middle line:90%
What color is it?

00:06:50.840 --> 00:06:51.590 align:middle line:90%
What's it made of?

00:06:51.590 --> 00:06:52.715 align:middle line:90%
How many legs does it have?

00:06:52.715 --> 00:06:53.510 align:middle line:90%
What shape is it?

00:06:53.510 --> 00:06:56.433 align:middle line:90%


00:06:56.433 --> 00:06:58.350 align:middle line:84%
So it turns out that
even something like "what

00:06:58.350 --> 00:07:01.590 align:middle line:84%
is a table" becomes really,
really challenging to answer

00:07:01.590 --> 00:07:02.768 align:middle line:90%
using an expert system.

00:07:02.768 --> 00:07:04.060 align:middle line:90%
So these can be really helpful.

00:07:04.060 --> 00:07:06.132 align:middle line:84%
And there are some simple
ways for their other--

00:07:06.132 --> 00:07:08.340 align:middle line:84%
there are some problems
where heuristics are actually

00:07:08.340 --> 00:07:09.060 align:middle line:90%
the best way to do it.

00:07:09.060 --> 00:07:11.560 align:middle line:84%
Because it's interpretable, you
know what's going to happen,

00:07:11.560 --> 00:07:14.900 align:middle line:84%
and you don't need a neural
network for every problem.

00:07:14.900 --> 00:07:19.790 align:middle line:84%
But this definitely can't solve
every problem [INAUDIBLE]..

00:07:19.790 --> 00:07:25.847 align:middle line:84%
Another kind of algorithm people
might use in classical AI,

00:07:25.847 --> 00:07:26.930 align:middle line:90%
it's called a tree search.

00:07:26.930 --> 00:07:28.310 align:middle line:90%
You start in your current state.

00:07:28.310 --> 00:07:29.900 align:middle line:84%
That's state A.
And you enumerate

00:07:29.900 --> 00:07:31.610 align:middle line:84%
all of the possible
options from there.

00:07:31.610 --> 00:07:34.220 align:middle line:84%
And then you just repeat
that for each state

00:07:34.220 --> 00:07:36.680 align:middle line:84%
until you find the
state where you want

00:07:36.680 --> 00:07:39.200 align:middle line:90%
to end up in-- your goal state.

00:07:39.200 --> 00:07:42.800 align:middle line:90%
Now, obvious problem here--

00:07:42.800 --> 00:07:43.980 align:middle line:90%
a lot of options.

00:07:43.980 --> 00:07:47.670 align:middle line:84%
A lot of options when you're
operating in the real world.

00:07:47.670 --> 00:07:49.545 align:middle line:84%
So how do you actually
enumerate all of them?

00:07:49.545 --> 00:07:51.878 align:middle line:84%
And sometimes, it can even
be difficult to define a goal

00:07:51.878 --> 00:07:52.410 align:middle line:90%
state.

00:07:52.410 --> 00:07:55.310 align:middle line:84%
When I'm playing chess, I know
there's a goal state of win--

00:07:55.310 --> 00:07:56.270 align:middle line:90%
checkmate.

00:07:56.270 --> 00:08:00.720 align:middle line:84%
But in real-life environments,
it's not so simple.

00:08:00.720 --> 00:08:03.440 align:middle line:84%
So again, can be
really, really helpful.

00:08:03.440 --> 00:08:09.530 align:middle line:84%
And this is how the chess
computers beat Garry Kasparov.

00:08:09.530 --> 00:08:13.110 align:middle line:90%
But also limited.

00:08:13.110 --> 00:08:16.290 align:middle line:90%


00:08:16.290 --> 00:08:18.750 align:middle line:84%
So now, we see these are just
two ways to implement AI.

00:08:18.750 --> 00:08:20.100 align:middle line:90%
They're not that smart.

00:08:20.100 --> 00:08:22.180 align:middle line:84%
Like, most of us could
have come up with these.

00:08:22.180 --> 00:08:24.300 align:middle line:84%
If we were just
told, like, come up

00:08:24.300 --> 00:08:26.070 align:middle line:84%
with a way to
solve this problem,

00:08:26.070 --> 00:08:29.520 align:middle line:84%
we probably would have figured
out that a list of rules

00:08:29.520 --> 00:08:31.260 align:middle line:84%
or enumerating all
of the options--

00:08:31.260 --> 00:08:33.340 align:middle line:90%
yeah, that makes sense.

00:08:33.340 --> 00:08:35.730 align:middle line:84%
So let's now move into
machine learning, which

00:08:35.730 --> 00:08:37.890 align:middle line:84%
is a subset of
artificial intelligence

00:08:37.890 --> 00:08:40.892 align:middle line:84%
that allows a machine to
learn automatically from data.

00:08:40.892 --> 00:08:42.600 align:middle line:84%
And this is what
somebody else mentioned.

00:08:42.600 --> 00:08:45.710 align:middle line:84%
So this is where there's not a
programmer who's sitting there

00:08:45.710 --> 00:08:48.600 align:middle line:84%
explicitly programming
or changing the code,

00:08:48.600 --> 00:08:49.730 align:middle line:90%
changing the model.

00:08:49.730 --> 00:08:51.230 align:middle line:84%
It's like oh, well,
if this happens,

00:08:51.230 --> 00:08:52.605 align:middle line:84%
then you should
actually do that.

00:08:52.605 --> 00:08:55.067 align:middle line:84%
It is learning automatically
by looking at data.

00:08:55.067 --> 00:08:57.150 align:middle line:84%
And I'll say more about
learning in just a second.

00:08:57.150 --> 00:08:59.025 align:middle line:84%
But let me just first
answer the question of,

00:08:59.025 --> 00:09:00.600 align:middle line:90%
when do we use machine learning?

00:09:00.600 --> 00:09:03.210 align:middle line:90%


00:09:03.210 --> 00:09:06.260 align:middle line:84%
So machine learning can
perform many different tasks.

00:09:06.260 --> 00:09:10.190 align:middle line:84%
Three of the most common are
classification, clustering,

00:09:10.190 --> 00:09:12.250 align:middle line:90%
and regression.

00:09:12.250 --> 00:09:16.150 align:middle line:84%
Classification means,
here's some input data.

00:09:16.150 --> 00:09:18.235 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to give you
an output of a class.

00:09:18.235 --> 00:09:21.700 align:middle line:90%


00:09:21.700 --> 00:09:25.520 align:middle line:84%
So maybe it's an image, and I
tell you what kind of animal

00:09:25.520 --> 00:09:26.020 align:middle line:90%
it is.

00:09:26.020 --> 00:09:27.978 align:middle line:84%
Maybe it's a list of
information about somebody

00:09:27.978 --> 00:09:30.928 align:middle line:84%
and I tell you whether
they're a threat or not.

00:09:30.928 --> 00:09:35.340 align:middle line:84%
You give me an input,
I give you a class.

00:09:35.340 --> 00:09:41.128 align:middle line:84%
Clustering is, again, you
give me some kind of input,

00:09:41.128 --> 00:09:42.420 align:middle line:90%
and I'm going to find clusters.

00:09:42.420 --> 00:09:44.010 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to find
things that are similar.

00:09:44.010 --> 00:09:45.960 align:middle line:84%
Maybe I'm clustering
different groups of animals

00:09:45.960 --> 00:09:47.793 align:middle line:84%
or other similar groups
of animals together.

00:09:47.793 --> 00:09:49.320 align:middle line:84%
Maybe it's a similar
gene sequences.

00:09:49.320 --> 00:09:51.195 align:middle line:90%
Oh, these look related somehow.

00:09:51.195 --> 00:09:54.130 align:middle line:90%


00:09:54.130 --> 00:09:58.030 align:middle line:84%
So clustering can be really
useful, especially in genetics,

00:09:58.030 --> 00:09:59.440 align:middle line:90%
from what I've heard--

00:09:59.440 --> 00:10:01.322 align:middle line:90%
not that I've ever done that.

00:10:01.322 --> 00:10:03.280 align:middle line:84%
And then another kind of
problem is regression.

00:10:03.280 --> 00:10:06.190 align:middle line:84%
And regression is, again,
you get input data.

00:10:06.190 --> 00:10:12.370 align:middle line:84%
But instead of putting a
class, like "dog" or "safe,"

00:10:12.370 --> 00:10:14.390 align:middle line:90%
you put a numerical prediction.

00:10:14.390 --> 00:10:17.050 align:middle line:84%
So maybe you're predicting home
price based on square footage.

00:10:17.050 --> 00:10:19.630 align:middle line:84%
Maybe you're predicting
crime rate based on location.

00:10:19.630 --> 00:10:22.520 align:middle line:90%


00:10:22.520 --> 00:10:23.510 align:middle line:90%
And in this example--

00:10:23.510 --> 00:10:25.677 align:middle line:84%
let me just ask you as a
question-- what's the model

00:10:25.677 --> 00:10:26.750 align:middle line:90%
that we're using?

00:10:26.750 --> 00:10:30.000 align:middle line:84%
What's the model the
machine-- technically,

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:31.508 align:middle line:90%
it's a machine learning model.

00:10:31.508 --> 00:10:32.550 align:middle line:90%
Or, you can call it that.

00:10:32.550 --> 00:10:35.100 align:middle line:84%
What's the model that we've
trained in this example?

00:10:35.100 --> 00:10:38.040 align:middle line:84%
What's actually
doing the prediction?

00:10:38.040 --> 00:10:40.664 align:middle line:84%
SPEAKER 1: Answer
from participant.

00:10:40.664 --> 00:10:42.100 align:middle line:90%
SPEAKER 2: The red line?

00:10:42.100 --> 00:10:43.350 align:middle line:90%
BRANDON LESHCHINSKIY: Exactly.

00:10:43.350 --> 00:10:44.780 align:middle line:90%
It's a line.

00:10:44.780 --> 00:10:47.280 align:middle line:84%
And I just want to say this,
because I think a lot of people

00:10:47.280 --> 00:10:48.988 align:middle line:84%
think models are like
really complicated.

00:10:48.988 --> 00:10:50.670 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes, the model
is just a line.

00:10:50.670 --> 00:10:51.870 align:middle line:90%
Like, this is a model.

00:10:51.870 --> 00:10:56.190 align:middle line:84%
I give it some kind of input,
and it gives me an output.

00:10:56.190 --> 00:10:57.350 align:middle line:90%
This is a model.

00:10:57.350 --> 00:10:59.842 align:middle line:90%
You can also use a curve.

00:10:59.842 --> 00:11:01.050 align:middle line:90%
You can use a neural network.

00:11:01.050 --> 00:11:03.880 align:middle line:84%
It's way more complicated--
a lot more operations there.

00:11:03.880 --> 00:11:05.370 align:middle line:90%
But these are all models.

00:11:05.370 --> 00:11:09.120 align:middle line:84%
So like I said, machine
learning can perform many tasks.

00:11:09.120 --> 00:11:11.430 align:middle line:84%
Classification,
clustering and regression

00:11:11.430 --> 00:11:13.742 align:middle line:90%
are the most commonly performed.

00:11:13.742 --> 00:11:16.200 align:middle line:84%
And now, I want to talk a little
bit about what it actually

00:11:16.200 --> 00:11:18.170 align:middle line:90%
means to learn something.

00:11:18.170 --> 00:11:20.070 align:middle line:84%
What does it mean for
a machine to learn?

00:11:20.070 --> 00:11:23.720 align:middle line:84%
Are there any questions
up until this point?

00:11:23.720 --> 00:11:26.455 align:middle line:84%
SPEAKER 2: Question
from participant:

00:11:26.455 --> 00:11:28.330 align:middle line:84%
SPEAKER 1: Where does
a convolutional network

00:11:28.330 --> 00:11:29.560 align:middle line:90%
fall in the scheme of things?

00:11:29.560 --> 00:11:31.810 align:middle line:84%
BRANDON LESHCHINSKIY: So
convolutional neural networks

00:11:31.810 --> 00:11:34.630 align:middle line:84%
are a very specific tool
that are used usually

00:11:34.630 --> 00:11:36.910 align:middle line:90%
in computer vision.

00:11:36.910 --> 00:11:38.170 align:middle line:90%
That is a model.

00:11:38.170 --> 00:11:44.900 align:middle line:84%
And we will-- let me get back
to convolutional neural networks

00:11:44.900 --> 00:11:45.900 align:middle line:90%
after this section.

00:11:45.900 --> 00:11:47.180 align:middle line:90%
I'll talk briefly about them.

00:11:47.180 --> 00:11:48.710 align:middle line:84%
So three types of
learning, guys.

00:11:48.710 --> 00:11:50.610 align:middle line:84%
There's supervised,
unsupervised,

00:11:50.610 --> 00:11:51.770 align:middle line:90%
and reinforcement learning.

00:11:51.770 --> 00:11:53.870 align:middle line:84%
When we talk about
supervised learning,

00:11:53.870 --> 00:11:55.483 align:middle line:84%
it's really like
studying for a test.

00:11:55.483 --> 00:11:56.900 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to give
you the example.

00:11:56.900 --> 00:11:59.085 align:middle line:84%
Sorry, I'm going to
give you the questions

00:11:59.085 --> 00:12:00.210 align:middle line:90%
and the answers in advance.

00:12:00.210 --> 00:12:00.920 align:middle line:90%
You're going to study.

00:12:00.920 --> 00:12:03.620 align:middle line:84%
Them you're going to figure out
what the broad patterns are.

00:12:03.620 --> 00:12:05.650 align:middle line:90%
And then I'm going to test you.

00:12:05.650 --> 00:12:07.360 align:middle line:84%
This is often--
doesn't have to be,

00:12:07.360 --> 00:12:09.790 align:middle line:84%
but it's often applied to
classification problems.

00:12:09.790 --> 00:12:12.400 align:middle line:84%
Now, the key thing here is
that, in supervised learning,

00:12:12.400 --> 00:12:14.067 align:middle line:84%
you need to have the
answers in advance.

00:12:14.067 --> 00:12:16.850 align:middle line:90%


00:12:16.850 --> 00:12:19.650 align:middle line:84%
You need to have labeled
data, in other words.

00:12:19.650 --> 00:12:22.040 align:middle line:84%
I need to-- it's not
enough for me to just show

00:12:22.040 --> 00:12:23.570 align:middle line:90%
the model a bunch of images.

00:12:23.570 --> 00:12:27.350 align:middle line:84%
The images have to be labeled
as cat, cow, giraffe, dog,

00:12:27.350 --> 00:12:28.130 align:middle line:90%
whatever.

00:12:28.130 --> 00:12:29.780 align:middle line:90%
Threat, not threat.

00:12:29.780 --> 00:12:30.870 align:middle line:90%
There have to be answers.

00:12:30.870 --> 00:12:32.287 align:middle line:84%
Otherwise, it'd
be like giving you

00:12:32.287 --> 00:12:33.920 align:middle line:90%
flashcards with only one side.

00:12:33.920 --> 00:12:37.760 align:middle line:84%
And you're turning it over, and
you're like, I have no idea.

00:12:37.760 --> 00:12:40.900 align:middle line:84%
So that's what
supervised learning is.

00:12:40.900 --> 00:12:44.950 align:middle line:84%
In unsupervised
learning, it's more--

00:12:44.950 --> 00:12:46.213 align:middle line:90%
you don't have those answers.

00:12:46.213 --> 00:12:47.380 align:middle line:90%
You don't have those labels.

00:12:47.380 --> 00:12:48.430 align:middle line:90%
It's more like a baby playing.

00:12:48.430 --> 00:12:49.930 align:middle line:84%
You're just
discovering patterns.

00:12:49.930 --> 00:12:52.030 align:middle line:84%
This is often used
in clustering.

00:12:52.030 --> 00:12:54.802 align:middle line:84%
I don't know, a priori,
what the right clusters

00:12:54.802 --> 00:12:55.760 align:middle line:90%
are going to look like.

00:12:55.760 --> 00:12:57.910 align:middle line:84%
I have no idea what
they should look like.

00:12:57.910 --> 00:13:00.415 align:middle line:84%
All I know is, oh,
these looks something--

00:13:00.415 --> 00:13:01.540 align:middle line:90%
they look somewhat similar.

00:13:01.540 --> 00:13:04.570 align:middle line:90%


00:13:04.570 --> 00:13:07.360 align:middle line:84%
These look somewhat similar,
and they look kind of different

00:13:07.360 --> 00:13:08.920 align:middle line:90%
from each other.

00:13:08.920 --> 00:13:11.530 align:middle line:84%
But I have no external
source of information

00:13:11.530 --> 00:13:14.440 align:middle line:84%
as far as what kind of
class this belongs to.

00:13:14.440 --> 00:13:17.400 align:middle line:90%
Does it make sense?

00:13:17.400 --> 00:13:19.080 align:middle line:90%
So that's unsupervised learning.

00:13:19.080 --> 00:13:20.810 align:middle line:84%
And then the last kind of
learning that we'll talk about

00:13:20.810 --> 00:13:21.907 align:middle line:90%
is reinforcement learning.

00:13:21.907 --> 00:13:23.990 align:middle line:84%
Reinforcement learning is,
again, you don't really

00:13:23.990 --> 00:13:26.690 align:middle line:84%
have a right answer, and you're
not really playing either.

00:13:26.690 --> 00:13:28.800 align:middle line:90%
It's something separate.

00:13:28.800 --> 00:13:31.790 align:middle line:84%
You are aiming to reward
some kind of behavior

00:13:31.790 --> 00:13:34.290 align:middle line:84%
or some kind of
solution to a problem.

00:13:34.290 --> 00:13:35.600 align:middle line:90%
It's like a rat in a maze.

00:13:35.600 --> 00:13:37.580 align:middle line:84%
You give it cheese,
and it discovers

00:13:37.580 --> 00:13:39.422 align:middle line:90%
winning strategies on its own.

00:13:39.422 --> 00:13:40.880 align:middle line:84%
So you don't have
the right answer.

00:13:40.880 --> 00:13:43.730 align:middle line:84%
You just know that you need the
thing to get out of the maze.

00:13:43.730 --> 00:13:46.370 align:middle line:84%
This is often used in
robotics, for example,

00:13:46.370 --> 00:13:53.040 align:middle line:84%
with training a robotic
arm, or maybe training

00:13:53.040 --> 00:13:53.880 align:middle line:90%
an agent in a game.

00:13:53.880 --> 00:13:57.390 align:middle line:90%


00:13:57.390 --> 00:14:01.200 align:middle line:84%
So supervised, unsupervised,
reinforcement learning.

00:14:01.200 --> 00:14:06.900 align:middle line:84%
These are the three ways to
learn in machine learning.

00:14:06.900 --> 00:14:10.160 align:middle line:84%
Any questions about
these three things?

00:14:10.160 --> 00:14:12.650 align:middle line:84%
SPEAKER 1: Question
from participant:

00:14:12.650 --> 00:14:14.150 align:middle line:84%
SPEAKER 2: Is
reinforcement learning

00:14:14.150 --> 00:14:16.280 align:middle line:84%
where we know what
we wanted to do

00:14:16.280 --> 00:14:18.920 align:middle line:84%
and technology figures that
avenues with parameters

00:14:18.920 --> 00:14:19.670 align:middle line:90%
to get there?

00:14:19.670 --> 00:14:21.480 align:middle line:90%


00:14:21.480 --> 00:14:22.730 align:middle line:90%
BRANDON LESHCHINSKIY: Exactly.

00:14:22.730 --> 00:14:25.520 align:middle line:90%


00:14:25.520 --> 00:14:33.310 align:middle line:84%
So you might, for example,
say, win this game,

00:14:33.310 --> 00:14:35.800 align:middle line:84%
and over the course of
a million simulations,

00:14:35.800 --> 00:14:39.317 align:middle line:84%
the thing learns, oh,
probabilistically, this

00:14:39.317 --> 00:14:40.900 align:middle line:84%
has a good chance
of winning the game.

00:14:40.900 --> 00:14:42.190 align:middle line:84%
This does not let
me do this thing.

00:14:42.190 --> 00:14:44.380 align:middle line:84%
Now, let me just actually
add about reinforcement

00:14:44.380 --> 00:14:46.840 align:middle line:84%
learning-- all of these
types of learnings

00:14:46.840 --> 00:14:50.350 align:middle line:90%
have pros and cons, of course.

00:14:50.350 --> 00:14:54.130 align:middle line:84%
So with supervised
learning, the trade-off

00:14:54.130 --> 00:14:57.580 align:middle line:84%
is, you need just, like, a
stupid amount of labeled data.

00:14:57.580 --> 00:14:58.840 align:middle line:90%
And then that takes forever.

00:14:58.840 --> 00:15:01.628 align:middle line:84%
That can be very, very expensive
to collect and label data.

00:15:01.628 --> 00:15:03.670 align:middle line:84%
With unsupervised learning,
you don't necessarily

00:15:03.670 --> 00:15:04.580 align:middle line:90%
know what the right answer is.

00:15:04.580 --> 00:15:06.250 align:middle line:84%
So it can help you
discover patterns,

00:15:06.250 --> 00:15:09.310 align:middle line:84%
but it may not be as powerful
as supervised learning.

00:15:09.310 --> 00:15:11.560 align:middle line:84%
And with reinforcement
learning, the real trick

00:15:11.560 --> 00:15:13.810 align:middle line:90%
is, how good is your simulation?

00:15:13.810 --> 00:15:17.260 align:middle line:84%
Because you're not going
to take a real car and do

00:15:17.260 --> 00:15:19.587 align:middle line:84%
reinforcement learning
with a real car.

00:15:19.587 --> 00:15:21.420 align:middle line:84%
It's going to take you
a million iterations.

00:15:21.420 --> 00:15:23.462 align:middle line:84%
You're going to have to
go through a million cars

00:15:23.462 --> 00:15:26.808 align:middle line:84%
before it learns how to
drive without crashing.

00:15:26.808 --> 00:15:28.600 align:middle line:84%
When you simulate it,
then the question is,

00:15:28.600 --> 00:15:30.740 align:middle line:90%
how good is your simulation?

00:15:30.740 --> 00:15:32.500 align:middle line:84%
Will it actually apply
to the real world?

00:15:32.500 --> 00:15:35.000 align:middle line:84%
Are you capturing all of the
noise, all of the disturbances,

00:15:35.000 --> 00:15:36.950 align:middle line:90%
all of the possibilities?

00:15:36.950 --> 00:15:41.160 align:middle line:84%
Are you capturing that biker
who cuts you off, or the wind,

00:15:41.160 --> 00:15:46.340 align:middle line:84%
or the reflection of
the sun on a white sign?

00:15:46.340 --> 00:15:50.300 align:middle line:84%
So there are pros and
cons to each of these.

00:15:50.300 --> 00:15:54.083 align:middle line:90%


00:15:54.083 --> 00:15:55.500 align:middle line:84%
So now, what we're
going to do is,

00:15:55.500 --> 00:15:58.785 align:middle line:84%
we're actually going to
train our own algorithm.

00:15:58.785 --> 00:16:00.660 align:middle line:84%
And again, I want to
reiterate the main point

00:16:00.660 --> 00:16:02.490 align:middle line:84%
of this presentation before
we get into that algorithm

00:16:02.490 --> 00:16:03.090 align:middle line:90%
training.

00:16:03.090 --> 00:16:04.757 align:middle line:84%
With the right data
and the right model,

00:16:04.757 --> 00:16:05.975 align:middle line:90%
AI can solve many problems.

00:16:05.975 --> 00:16:08.100 align:middle line:84%
Choosing the right problem,
finding the right data,

00:16:08.100 --> 00:16:14.330 align:middle line:84%
training the right model are
all potentially difficult.

00:16:14.330 --> 00:16:18.008 align:middle line:90%
So let's do this.

00:16:18.008 --> 00:16:19.550 align:middle line:84%
We're going to walk
through the seven

00:16:19.550 --> 00:16:21.290 align:middle line:90%
steps of training an algorithm.

00:16:21.290 --> 00:16:23.370 align:middle line:90%
Step one is, define a problem.

00:16:23.370 --> 00:16:24.810 align:middle line:84%
Now, here, I've
defined it for us.

00:16:24.810 --> 00:16:27.018 align:middle line:84%
I've said, we're going to
decide whether a picture is

00:16:27.018 --> 00:16:27.968 align:middle line:90%
a cat or a dog.

00:16:27.968 --> 00:16:30.260 align:middle line:84%
So first, let me ask you,
what kind of machine learning

00:16:30.260 --> 00:16:30.980 align:middle line:90%
problem is this?

00:16:30.980 --> 00:16:34.120 align:middle line:90%


00:16:34.120 --> 00:16:36.490 align:middle line:84%
SPEAKER 1: Answer
from participant:

00:16:36.490 --> 00:16:37.905 align:middle line:90%
SPEAKER 2: Classification.

00:16:37.905 --> 00:16:39.030 align:middle line:90%
BRANDON LESHCHINSKIY: Cool.

00:16:39.030 --> 00:16:39.780 align:middle line:90%
Everybody see why?

00:16:39.780 --> 00:16:43.488 align:middle line:90%


00:16:43.488 --> 00:16:44.780 align:middle line:90%
I'm getting some kind of input.

00:16:44.780 --> 00:16:45.655 align:middle line:90%
I'm getting an image.

00:16:45.655 --> 00:16:47.030 align:middle line:84%
And I'm telling
you what class it

00:16:47.030 --> 00:16:48.260 align:middle line:90%
is, what class it belongs to.

00:16:48.260 --> 00:16:49.385 align:middle line:90%
It's either a cat or a dog.

00:16:49.385 --> 00:16:53.690 align:middle line:90%


00:16:53.690 --> 00:16:56.390 align:middle line:84%
Now, in this case, the work
of problem identification

00:16:56.390 --> 00:16:58.147 align:middle line:84%
has been done for us,
and we're assuming

00:16:58.147 --> 00:16:59.730 align:middle line:84%
that classifying
between cats and dogs

00:16:59.730 --> 00:17:02.150 align:middle line:84%
is actually closing
an important gap.

00:17:02.150 --> 00:17:05.510 align:middle line:84%
However, let me just say,
problem identification

00:17:05.510 --> 00:17:07.589 align:middle line:90%
is super important.

00:17:07.589 --> 00:17:11.930 align:middle line:84%
There are a lot of problems that
cannot be solved by AI, period.

00:17:11.930 --> 00:17:15.060 align:middle line:84%
If you have issues with
diversity in your unit,

00:17:15.060 --> 00:17:18.270 align:middle line:84%
machine learning
is not the answer.

00:17:18.270 --> 00:17:21.462 align:middle line:84%
But as with any new technology,
it's really seductive.

00:17:21.462 --> 00:17:22.170 align:middle line:90%
It's really sexy.

00:17:22.170 --> 00:17:24.128 align:middle line:84%
Everybody wants to be
like, oh, we're using AI.

00:17:24.128 --> 00:17:27.210 align:middle line:84%
So I just want to
make clear that it's

00:17:27.210 --> 00:17:31.617 align:middle line:84%
important to be discerning
with where you even apply AI.

00:17:31.617 --> 00:17:33.450 align:middle line:84%
And that comes into
that very first step of,

00:17:33.450 --> 00:17:36.180 align:middle line:84%
what's the problem that
we're trying to solve?

00:17:36.180 --> 00:17:37.010 align:middle line:90%
Define the problem.

00:17:37.010 --> 00:17:40.640 align:middle line:90%


00:17:40.640 --> 00:17:43.280 align:middle line:84%
Step two is, cool,
I have a problem.

00:17:43.280 --> 00:17:44.590 align:middle line:90%
Find data.

00:17:44.590 --> 00:17:48.230 align:middle line:84%
What kind of data do I need in
order to solve this problem?

00:17:48.230 --> 00:17:55.673 align:middle line:84%
So let's say I wanted to train
an algorithm that can classify

00:17:55.673 --> 00:17:56.590 align:middle line:90%
between cats and dogs.

00:17:56.590 --> 00:18:00.100 align:middle line:84%
What kind of data
should I look at?

00:18:00.100 --> 00:18:02.990 align:middle line:84%
SPEAKER 2: Answer
from participant:

00:18:02.990 --> 00:18:05.300 align:middle line:84%
SPEAKER 1: It's going to be
a lot of visual data, lots

00:18:05.300 --> 00:18:08.120 align:middle line:84%
of images to show cats can look
all of these different ways,

00:18:08.120 --> 00:18:11.200 align:middle line:84%
dogs can look all of
these different ways.

00:18:11.200 --> 00:18:13.720 align:middle line:84%
BRANDON LESHCHINSKIY:
So something like this.

00:18:13.720 --> 00:18:15.280 align:middle line:90%
Exactly.

00:18:15.280 --> 00:18:16.690 align:middle line:90%
Now, this data has labels.

00:18:16.690 --> 00:18:19.000 align:middle line:84%
So what kind of learning are
we able to do as a result?

00:18:19.000 --> 00:18:23.010 align:middle line:90%


00:18:23.010 --> 00:18:25.892 align:middle line:84%
SPEAKER 2: Answer
from participant.

00:18:25.892 --> 00:18:27.382 align:middle line:90%
SPEAKER 1: Supervised learning.

00:18:27.382 --> 00:18:28.465 align:middle line:90%
BRANDON LESHCHINSKIY: Yes.

00:18:28.465 --> 00:18:30.537 align:middle line:90%


00:18:30.537 --> 00:18:32.370 align:middle line:84%
Because we have the
right answer in advance.

00:18:32.370 --> 00:18:34.607 align:middle line:84%
So we can train it and
say, ah, this is a cat.

00:18:34.607 --> 00:18:35.190 align:middle line:90%
This is a dog.

00:18:35.190 --> 00:18:38.298 align:middle line:84%
Now, if we didn't have labels,
what our algorithm might do

00:18:38.298 --> 00:18:39.840 align:middle line:84%
is cluster them
together and say, oh,

00:18:39.840 --> 00:18:42.257 align:middle line:84%
there's this type of thing and
there's this type of thing,

00:18:42.257 --> 00:18:44.320 align:middle line:90%
and they look different.

00:18:44.320 --> 00:18:45.820 align:middle line:84%
But there's no
external reference.

00:18:45.820 --> 00:18:47.695 align:middle line:84%
The thing has no idea
what a cat or a dog is.

00:18:47.695 --> 00:18:49.913 align:middle line:84%
It's just saying, these
two things look different.

00:18:49.913 --> 00:18:51.330 align:middle line:84%
Everybody see the
difference there

00:18:51.330 --> 00:18:53.500 align:middle line:84%
between supervised
and unsupervised?

00:18:53.500 --> 00:18:55.500 align:middle line:84%
So this way, we actually
can say, this is a cat.

00:18:55.500 --> 00:18:57.820 align:middle line:90%
This is a dog.

00:18:57.820 --> 00:19:00.228 align:middle line:84%
SPEAKER 1: Question
from participant:

00:19:00.228 --> 00:19:02.270 align:middle line:84%
SPEAKER 1: Is this how
Google and other companies

00:19:02.270 --> 00:19:05.150 align:middle line:84%
have been developing their
technology, using humans

00:19:05.150 --> 00:19:07.588 align:middle line:90%
to develop data pools?

00:19:07.588 --> 00:19:09.130 align:middle line:84%
BRANDON LESHCHINSKIY:
Yeah, there's--

00:19:09.130 --> 00:19:11.710 align:middle line:84%
I don't if you guys-- if anybody
here is familiar with XKCD.

00:19:11.710 --> 00:19:13.360 align:middle line:90%
It's an online webcomic.

00:19:13.360 --> 00:19:18.880 align:middle line:84%
There's one where it's like,
it's a Google sign-in thing,

00:19:18.880 --> 00:19:22.720 align:middle line:84%
and it's like, please find the
picture with the stop sign.

00:19:22.720 --> 00:19:24.550 align:middle line:84%
Next frame-- please
do it quickly.

00:19:24.550 --> 00:19:26.260 align:middle line:84%
Our car is approaching
the intersection.

00:19:26.260 --> 00:19:29.470 align:middle line:84%
So yes, this is absolutely
them collecting training data

00:19:29.470 --> 00:19:31.434 align:middle line:90%
by having humans label it.

00:19:31.434 --> 00:19:34.600 align:middle line:90%


00:19:34.600 --> 00:19:38.520 align:middle line:84%
So the next step after we've
found data is clean data.

00:19:38.520 --> 00:19:41.190 align:middle line:84%
And I know this feels
like, maybe, extraneous.

00:19:41.190 --> 00:19:44.640 align:middle line:84%
But for anybody here who
has worked with data before,

00:19:44.640 --> 00:19:49.860 align:middle line:84%
you get this is, like, 80% of
data science and 80% of AI.

00:19:49.860 --> 00:19:53.930 align:middle line:84%
Cleaning data is
the biggest pain,

00:19:53.930 --> 00:19:55.910 align:middle line:90%
but it's most of the work.

00:19:55.910 --> 00:19:58.128 align:middle line:84%
It's because data doesn't
just exist in this nice way

00:19:58.128 --> 00:20:00.170 align:middle line:84%
where you can show it to
a machine learning model

00:20:00.170 --> 00:20:01.128 align:middle line:90%
and it starts learning.

00:20:01.128 --> 00:20:03.975 align:middle line:84%
Like, data exists in some
weird directory on the internet

00:20:03.975 --> 00:20:06.350 align:middle line:84%
where you have to download
it, you have to reorganize it,

00:20:06.350 --> 00:20:08.000 align:middle line:84%
you have to name
it properly, you

00:20:08.000 --> 00:20:10.922 align:middle line:84%
have to make sure that
there are no errors there,

00:20:10.922 --> 00:20:13.130 align:middle line:84%
you have to make sure that
you can access it quickly,

00:20:13.130 --> 00:20:14.670 align:middle line:90%
blah, blah, blah.

00:20:14.670 --> 00:20:17.840 align:middle line:84%
So cleaning data is
a huge part of what

00:20:17.840 --> 00:20:21.050 align:middle line:84%
it means to be a data scientist
and to do machine learning

00:20:21.050 --> 00:20:23.330 align:middle line:90%
or to do AI.

00:20:23.330 --> 00:20:26.727 align:middle line:84%
And that's why if you have
already clean data, oh, my god,

00:20:26.727 --> 00:20:28.310 align:middle line:84%
it's like a data
scientist's paradise,

00:20:28.310 --> 00:20:29.450 align:middle line:84%
because they don't
have to do that work.

00:20:29.450 --> 00:20:31.658 align:middle line:84%
They can just go and download
stuff and train models,

00:20:31.658 --> 00:20:32.780 align:middle line:90%
and that's the fun part.

00:20:32.780 --> 00:20:36.350 align:middle line:90%


00:20:36.350 --> 00:20:40.480 align:middle line:84%
So getting good, clean
data is a huge boon

00:20:40.480 --> 00:20:41.830 align:middle line:90%
for any kind of AI development.

00:20:41.830 --> 00:20:45.340 align:middle line:84%
For us, we're going to say
that this is now clean.

00:20:45.340 --> 00:20:49.180 align:middle line:90%


00:20:49.180 --> 00:20:52.500 align:middle line:90%
Step four is, choose a model.

00:20:52.500 --> 00:20:54.510 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to show
you guys a model.

00:20:54.510 --> 00:20:57.193 align:middle line:84%
Now, don't get
attached to this model.

00:20:57.193 --> 00:20:58.110 align:middle line:90%
It's not a good model.

00:20:58.110 --> 00:21:00.250 align:middle line:84%
It's not a helpful
model in the real world.

00:21:00.250 --> 00:21:02.333 align:middle line:84%
It's a model that we're
going to use to illustrate

00:21:02.333 --> 00:21:04.090 align:middle line:90%
a few key concepts.

00:21:04.090 --> 00:21:05.760 align:middle line:90%
So this is our model.

00:21:05.760 --> 00:21:08.730 align:middle line:84%
Dogs always blank,
sometimes blank.

00:21:08.730 --> 00:21:13.440 align:middle line:90%
Cats-- always, sometimes.

00:21:13.440 --> 00:21:15.585 align:middle line:90%
This model is interpretable.

00:21:15.585 --> 00:21:17.460 align:middle line:84%
It means that it may
not be good, we'll find.

00:21:17.460 --> 00:21:20.002 align:middle line:84%
But at the very least, we'll be
able to know what it's doing.

00:21:20.002 --> 00:21:21.582 align:middle line:84%
It's not going to
always be true.

00:21:21.582 --> 00:21:23.290 align:middle line:84%
But this is the model
we're going to use.

00:21:23.290 --> 00:21:26.010 align:middle line:90%


00:21:26.010 --> 00:21:27.240 align:middle line:90%
So now, let's train it.

00:21:27.240 --> 00:21:29.738 align:middle line:84%
Let's see what it means for
our model to learn something.

00:21:29.738 --> 00:21:31.280 align:middle line:84%
We're going to show
the model inputs,

00:21:31.280 --> 00:21:35.860 align:middle line:84%
and we're going to ask
it, what is this thing?

00:21:35.860 --> 00:21:38.665 align:middle line:84%
If it answers them
correctly, model's great.

00:21:38.665 --> 00:21:39.790 align:middle line:90%
Doesn't need to be updated.

00:21:39.790 --> 00:21:42.290 align:middle line:84%
If it gets it wrong, maybe we
can improve the model somehow.

00:21:42.290 --> 00:21:47.120 align:middle line:84%
So at the very beginning, we're
going to show this picture.

00:21:47.120 --> 00:21:50.690 align:middle line:84%
Now, the model has never
seen anything at this point.

00:21:50.690 --> 00:21:52.190 align:middle line:90%
It's a totally blank slate.

00:21:52.190 --> 00:21:53.815 align:middle line:84%
It's just going to
make a random guess.

00:21:53.815 --> 00:21:55.810 align:middle line:90%


00:21:55.810 --> 00:21:58.390 align:middle line:90%
So obviously, this is a cat.

00:21:58.390 --> 00:21:59.650 align:middle line:90%
We know that.

00:21:59.650 --> 00:22:01.510 align:middle line:84%
If the model guessed
correctly, there

00:22:01.510 --> 00:22:03.190 align:middle line:90%
would be no update to the model.

00:22:03.190 --> 00:22:06.130 align:middle line:84%
As far as the model is
concerned, I'm 100% accurate.

00:22:06.130 --> 00:22:08.825 align:middle line:90%
Why would I change?

00:22:08.825 --> 00:22:10.700 align:middle line:84%
Well, let's say that
the model gets it wrong.

00:22:10.700 --> 00:22:12.680 align:middle line:90%
Let's say the model guessed dog.

00:22:12.680 --> 00:22:16.650 align:middle line:84%
Then in that case, we would
go ahead and update the model.

00:22:16.650 --> 00:22:19.303 align:middle line:84%
So again, we have, model
now knows it's a cat.

00:22:19.303 --> 00:22:20.470 align:middle line:90%
It's going to update itself.

00:22:20.470 --> 00:22:22.500 align:middle line:84%
So what should the model
now say about cats?

00:22:22.500 --> 00:22:24.750 align:middle line:84%
What does the model-- based
on this example, what does

00:22:24.750 --> 00:22:27.870 align:middle line:90%
the model now know about cats?

00:22:27.870 --> 00:22:30.565 align:middle line:84%
SPEAKER 2: Answers
from participants:

00:22:30.565 --> 00:22:32.920 align:middle line:84%
SPEAKER 1: Are you looking
for things like pointy ears,

00:22:32.920 --> 00:22:35.520 align:middle line:90%
pink nose, round eyes?

00:22:35.520 --> 00:22:37.680 align:middle line:84%
SPEAKER 2: I feel like
there's not even enough data

00:22:37.680 --> 00:22:39.360 align:middle line:90%
to say that yet.

00:22:39.360 --> 00:22:41.550 align:middle line:84%
With such a small training
set, all the model

00:22:41.550 --> 00:22:44.752 align:middle line:84%
knows is that a cat
looks like that.

00:22:44.752 --> 00:22:45.960 align:middle line:90%
BRANDON LESHCHINSKIY: Ah, OK.

00:22:45.960 --> 00:22:51.260 align:middle line:84%
So let me ask--
so good intuition.

00:22:51.260 --> 00:22:55.360 align:middle line:84%
So we heard pointy ears,
multicolor, laying on a bed,

00:22:55.360 --> 00:22:59.440 align:middle line:90%
maybe, paws.

00:22:59.440 --> 00:23:01.900 align:middle line:84%
In our model--
let me update it--

00:23:01.900 --> 00:23:04.850 align:middle line:84%
are we going to say
always have those things--

00:23:04.850 --> 00:23:06.850 align:middle line:84%
cats always have those
things, or cats sometimes

00:23:06.850 --> 00:23:07.600 align:middle line:90%
have those things?

00:23:07.600 --> 00:23:10.300 align:middle line:84%
Do cats always have pointed
ears and are on a bed

00:23:10.300 --> 00:23:13.150 align:middle line:84%
and have a pink nose,
or is it sometimes?

00:23:13.150 --> 00:23:15.560 align:middle line:90%
What do we think?

00:23:15.560 --> 00:23:18.155 align:middle line:84%
SPEAKER 1: Answer
from participant:

00:23:18.155 --> 00:23:20.828 align:middle line:84%
SPEAKER 2: Sometimes,
definitely.

00:23:20.828 --> 00:23:21.870 align:middle line:90%
BRANDON LESHCHINSKIY: OK.

00:23:21.870 --> 00:23:22.950 align:middle line:90%
Any different opinions?

00:23:22.950 --> 00:23:30.857 align:middle line:90%


00:23:30.857 --> 00:23:31.690 align:middle line:90%
So here's the thing.

00:23:31.690 --> 00:23:34.450 align:middle line:90%
Corey knows what cats are.

00:23:34.450 --> 00:23:35.360 align:middle line:90%
Our model is dumb.

00:23:35.360 --> 00:23:37.027 align:middle line:84%
Our model knows nothing
about the world.

00:23:37.027 --> 00:23:39.260 align:middle line:90%
It knows nothing about cats.

00:23:39.260 --> 00:23:42.700 align:middle line:84%
As far as the
model is concerned,

00:23:42.700 --> 00:23:45.520 align:middle line:84%
as somebody else
just mentioned, this

00:23:45.520 --> 00:23:46.990 align:middle line:90%
is the only cat in the world.

00:23:46.990 --> 00:23:48.670 align:middle line:84%
This is what every
cat looks like.

00:23:48.670 --> 00:23:52.330 align:middle line:84%
Every cat is this cat,
and this cat is every cat.

00:23:52.330 --> 00:23:54.900 align:middle line:90%


00:23:54.900 --> 00:23:57.600 align:middle line:84%
So from the model's
perspective, cats always

00:23:57.600 --> 00:23:59.073 align:middle line:90%
look exactly like this thing.

00:23:59.073 --> 00:24:00.990 align:middle line:84%
It's the only example
it's ever seen of a cat.

00:24:00.990 --> 00:24:02.480 align:middle line:90%
What do you want?

00:24:02.480 --> 00:24:06.600 align:middle line:90%
This is what cats look like.

00:24:06.600 --> 00:24:08.100 align:middle line:84%
So the model is
purely statistical.

00:24:08.100 --> 00:24:10.230 align:middle line:84%
It has no idea what a
cat is conceptually.

00:24:10.230 --> 00:24:13.290 align:middle line:84%
It just says, statistically
speaking, 100% of cats

00:24:13.290 --> 00:24:14.910 align:middle line:90%
look exactly like this cat.

00:24:14.910 --> 00:24:17.220 align:middle line:84%
Now, we show it a
second input example.

00:24:17.220 --> 00:24:20.050 align:middle line:90%


00:24:20.050 --> 00:24:20.550 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:24:20.550 --> 00:24:23.370 align:middle line:84%
Now, at this point, our
model's looking at this image.

00:24:23.370 --> 00:24:28.498 align:middle line:84%
It's saying, well, all of
the cats that I've seen

00:24:28.498 --> 00:24:30.290 align:middle line:84%
look like that other
cat, and this does not

00:24:30.290 --> 00:24:32.190 align:middle line:84%
look like that other cat,
so this must be a dog.

00:24:32.190 --> 00:24:32.960 align:middle line:90%
I haven't seen a dog yet.

00:24:32.960 --> 00:24:33.918 align:middle line:90%
This is probably a dog.

00:24:33.918 --> 00:24:36.450 align:middle line:90%


00:24:36.450 --> 00:24:37.470 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:24:37.470 --> 00:24:39.278 align:middle line:90%
Turns out, model's wrong.

00:24:39.278 --> 00:24:40.320 align:middle line:90%
Model has to get updated.

00:24:40.320 --> 00:24:44.840 align:middle line:90%


00:24:44.840 --> 00:24:49.118 align:middle line:84%
So at this point, what
is our model going

00:24:49.118 --> 00:24:50.410 align:middle line:90%
to-- so tell me about this cat.

00:24:50.410 --> 00:24:55.510 align:middle line:84%
What is the model going to know
about cats after this example?

00:24:55.510 --> 00:24:58.065 align:middle line:84%
SPEAKER 1: Answers
from participants:

00:24:58.065 --> 00:25:00.240 align:middle line:90%
SPEAKER 2: They're mean-looking.

00:25:00.240 --> 00:25:03.965 align:middle line:84%
Cats can be super fluffy
and have yellow eyes.

00:25:03.965 --> 00:25:06.025 align:middle line:84%
SPEAKER 1: Their pupils
can be small or big.

00:25:06.025 --> 00:25:07.150 align:middle line:90%
BRANDON LESHCHINSKIY: Good.

00:25:07.150 --> 00:25:07.650 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:25:07.650 --> 00:25:09.880 align:middle line:84%
So now, let me ask,
are we going to put

00:25:09.880 --> 00:25:14.050 align:middle line:84%
this in sometimes or always,
these examples we just got?

00:25:14.050 --> 00:25:17.000 align:middle line:84%
SPEAKER 2: Answer
from participant:

00:25:17.000 --> 00:25:18.590 align:middle line:90%
SPEAKER 1: Sometimes.

00:25:18.590 --> 00:25:18.950 align:middle line:84%
BRANDON LESHCHINSKIY:
Some of them

00:25:18.950 --> 00:25:20.325 align:middle line:84%
are going to be
sometimes, right?

00:25:20.325 --> 00:25:22.730 align:middle line:84%
So cats still have
two eyes, always--

00:25:22.730 --> 00:25:23.750 align:middle line:90%
so far.

00:25:23.750 --> 00:25:24.830 align:middle line:90%
All cats have two eyes.

00:25:24.830 --> 00:25:27.350 align:middle line:84%
All cats have a nose that
looks roughly like this.

00:25:27.350 --> 00:25:29.000 align:middle line:90%
Sometimes, it's a pink nose.

00:25:29.000 --> 00:25:30.350 align:middle line:90%
Sometimes, it's a black nose.

00:25:30.350 --> 00:25:34.050 align:middle line:84%
So now, we are fleshing out
our model a little bit more

00:25:34.050 --> 00:25:36.980 align:middle line:84%
and we're seeing, oh, these are
things that cats always have

00:25:36.980 --> 00:25:39.670 align:middle line:84%
and these are things
that cats sometimes have.

00:25:39.670 --> 00:25:42.870 align:middle line:90%
Does this make sense?

00:25:42.870 --> 00:25:45.212 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:25:45.212 --> 00:25:46.920 align:middle line:84%
All of the things that
we just wrote down

00:25:46.920 --> 00:25:48.000 align:middle line:90%
are called features--

00:25:48.000 --> 00:25:50.800 align:middle line:90%


00:25:50.800 --> 00:25:56.230 align:middle line:84%
the number of eyes,
the shape of the ears,

00:25:56.230 --> 00:25:57.490 align:middle line:90%
the size of the pupils.

00:25:57.490 --> 00:25:59.590 align:middle line:90%
These are all features.

00:25:59.590 --> 00:26:03.165 align:middle line:90%
Features are what allow us--

00:26:03.165 --> 00:26:05.165 align:middle line:84%
or allow the model,
rather-- to make a decision.

00:26:05.165 --> 00:26:07.870 align:middle line:90%


00:26:07.870 --> 00:26:11.230 align:middle line:84%
So for example,
if I am looking--

00:26:11.230 --> 00:26:15.460 align:middle line:84%
I'm just walking around,
trying to assess safety,

00:26:15.460 --> 00:26:17.470 align:middle line:84%
which is, I think,
a thing that people

00:26:17.470 --> 00:26:19.233 align:middle line:90%
kind of do instinctually.

00:26:19.233 --> 00:26:20.900 align:middle line:84%
There are features
that I'm looking for.

00:26:20.900 --> 00:26:24.280 align:middle line:84%
Like, is the person
that's approaching

00:26:24.280 --> 00:26:25.417 align:middle line:90%
me holding any weapons?

00:26:25.417 --> 00:26:26.500 align:middle line:90%
What's their posture like?

00:26:26.500 --> 00:26:29.620 align:middle line:84%
What's their facial
expression like?

00:26:29.620 --> 00:26:36.470 align:middle line:84%
I'm not looking at the
color of their hair.

00:26:36.470 --> 00:26:38.180 align:middle line:84%
Like, that's not
a relevant feature

00:26:38.180 --> 00:26:40.950 align:middle line:90%
in that particular example.

00:26:40.950 --> 00:26:42.737 align:middle line:84%
So in this case,
all of the things

00:26:42.737 --> 00:26:44.570 align:middle line:84%
that we're pulling out
to say-- to identify,

00:26:44.570 --> 00:26:49.160 align:middle line:84%
this is a cat or a dog, those
are what we call features.

00:26:49.160 --> 00:26:53.150 align:middle line:84%
Now, let me ask you a
somewhat tricky question.

00:26:53.150 --> 00:26:55.170 align:middle line:90%
When a computer sees an image--

00:26:55.170 --> 00:26:58.617 align:middle line:84%
let's say it's a 256
by 256-pixel image--

00:26:58.617 --> 00:27:00.200 align:middle line:84%
how many features
does a computer see?

00:27:00.200 --> 00:27:03.800 align:middle line:90%


00:27:03.800 --> 00:27:05.825 align:middle line:84%
SPEAKER 1: Answer
from participant:

00:27:05.825 --> 00:27:07.400 align:middle line:84%
SPEAKER 2: Is it
256, because that's

00:27:07.400 --> 00:27:10.270 align:middle line:90%
how many pixels there are?

00:27:10.270 --> 00:27:12.575 align:middle line:84%
BRANDON LESHCHINSKIY: So
it's 256 squared pixels.

00:27:12.575 --> 00:27:14.200 align:middle line:84%
And that's how many
features there are.

00:27:14.200 --> 00:27:15.450 align:middle line:90%
The computer is looking at it.

00:27:15.450 --> 00:27:16.570 align:middle line:90%
It's not looking at eyes.

00:27:16.570 --> 00:27:17.470 align:middle line:90%
It's not looking at ears.

00:27:17.470 --> 00:27:18.553 align:middle line:90%
It's not looking at notes.

00:27:18.553 --> 00:27:20.440 align:middle line:84%
It's looking at,
there's a pixel here,

00:27:20.440 --> 00:27:24.398 align:middle line:84%
and there's a whole bunch
more throughout the rest

00:27:24.398 --> 00:27:24.940 align:middle line:90%
of the image.

00:27:24.940 --> 00:27:26.410 align:middle line:84%
It's looking at
an array of data.

00:27:26.410 --> 00:27:29.410 align:middle line:90%


00:27:29.410 --> 00:27:31.600 align:middle line:84%
Now, what a neural
network does, and what

00:27:31.600 --> 00:27:33.280 align:middle line:84%
a convolutional
neural network does

00:27:33.280 --> 00:27:40.587 align:middle line:84%
in particular, is the
image gets passed-- so,

00:27:40.587 --> 00:27:42.670 align:middle line:84%
whoever asked about
convolutional neural networks,

00:27:42.670 --> 00:27:47.628 align:middle line:84%
if you weren't paying attention,
now's a good chance to tune in.

00:27:47.628 --> 00:27:50.170 align:middle line:84%
What a neural network does-- a
convolutional neural network--

00:27:50.170 --> 00:27:54.420 align:middle line:84%
takes an image, says
256 by 256 pixels.

00:27:54.420 --> 00:27:57.330 align:middle line:84%
Passes it through the first
layer of the neural network.

00:27:57.330 --> 00:27:58.455 align:middle line:90%
Starts extracting features.

00:27:58.455 --> 00:27:59.830 align:middle line:84%
With that first
layer, maybe it's

00:27:59.830 --> 00:28:04.080 align:middle line:84%
looking at lines, or
edges, or color gradients.

00:28:04.080 --> 00:28:09.070 align:middle line:84%
Passes it through to the next
layer in the neural network,

00:28:09.070 --> 00:28:11.687 align:middle line:84%
and it combines those
lines into shapes.

00:28:11.687 --> 00:28:13.770 align:middle line:84%
Maybe now, it's looking
for circles, or triangles,

00:28:13.770 --> 00:28:15.260 align:middle line:90%
or whatever.

00:28:15.260 --> 00:28:17.052 align:middle line:90%
Next part of the neural network.

00:28:17.052 --> 00:28:18.260 align:middle line:90%
It gets passed through again.

00:28:18.260 --> 00:28:20.510 align:middle line:84%
So it's processing it,
processing it, making it more--

00:28:20.510 --> 00:28:22.850 align:middle line:84%
like, making the features--
automatically extracting

00:28:22.850 --> 00:28:25.520 align:middle line:84%
features at each step, and
making the features more

00:28:25.520 --> 00:28:26.700 align:middle line:90%
and more sophisticated.

00:28:26.700 --> 00:28:28.400 align:middle line:84%
So now, it's looking
at noses, and eyes,

00:28:28.400 --> 00:28:29.380 align:middle line:84%
and now finally,
at the very end,

00:28:29.380 --> 00:28:31.550 align:middle line:84%
it's looking at a whole
face or a whole body.

00:28:31.550 --> 00:28:34.265 align:middle line:90%


00:28:34.265 --> 00:28:36.390 align:middle line:84%
So that's what a convolutional
neural network does.

00:28:36.390 --> 00:28:38.520 align:middle line:84%
But it starts, with the
model's perspective,

00:28:38.520 --> 00:28:42.150 align:middle line:90%
as 256 by 256 features.

00:28:42.150 --> 00:28:45.220 align:middle line:84%
And what a neural network
is sometimes really,

00:28:45.220 --> 00:28:47.760 align:middle line:84%
really good at is extracting
the features that are relevant,

00:28:47.760 --> 00:28:49.400 align:middle line:84%
combining them,
and processing them

00:28:49.400 --> 00:28:51.150 align:middle line:84%
until they're more and
more sophisticated.

00:28:51.150 --> 00:28:53.370 align:middle line:84%
And now, you have facial
recognition, for example.

00:28:53.370 --> 00:28:56.743 align:middle line:84%
There's, like, some kind
of eye/nose/mouth structure

00:28:56.743 --> 00:28:57.660 align:middle line:90%
that it's looking for.

00:28:57.660 --> 00:28:59.010 align:middle line:90%
Oh, this is your face.

00:28:59.010 --> 00:29:00.525 align:middle line:90%
I know who this is.

00:29:00.525 --> 00:29:02.650 align:middle line:84%
So that's what a convolutional
neural network does.

00:29:02.650 --> 00:29:06.993 align:middle line:84%
But I just want to be clear,
again, models are dumb.

00:29:06.993 --> 00:29:09.160 align:middle line:84%
They're not looking at this
from the perspective of,

00:29:09.160 --> 00:29:11.563 align:middle line:84%
I know what a nose
or a cat or a dog is.

00:29:11.563 --> 00:29:13.480 align:middle line:84%
They're looking at it
from the perspective of,

00:29:13.480 --> 00:29:15.070 align:middle line:90%
I see an array of data.

00:29:15.070 --> 00:29:17.670 align:middle line:90%


00:29:17.670 --> 00:29:20.460 align:middle line:84%
And sometimes, a neural
network, a model,

00:29:20.460 --> 00:29:22.710 align:middle line:84%
can interpret it and
create these features

00:29:22.710 --> 00:29:23.940 align:middle line:90%
that are more sophisticated.

00:29:23.940 --> 00:29:26.080 align:middle line:84%
I'll also say, these
models can be very brittle.

00:29:26.080 --> 00:29:28.648 align:middle line:84%
So there was a paper written
a few years ago where they

00:29:28.648 --> 00:29:30.190 align:middle line:84%
basically tricked
the neural network,

00:29:30.190 --> 00:29:33.223 align:middle line:84%
and by changing just a few
pixels or adding some noise,

00:29:33.223 --> 00:29:34.890 align:middle line:84%
it still looks like
a turtle to humans--

00:29:34.890 --> 00:29:36.390 align:middle line:90%
we still say it's a turtle--

00:29:36.390 --> 00:29:40.328 align:middle line:84%
but the model looks at it
and says, oh, is a gun,

00:29:40.328 --> 00:29:41.370 align:middle line:90%
or oh, this is a rooster.

00:29:41.370 --> 00:29:43.955 align:middle line:90%


00:29:43.955 --> 00:29:45.830 align:middle line:84%
And they could change
anything into anything,

00:29:45.830 --> 00:29:48.075 align:middle line:84%
as far as the model
was concerned.

00:29:48.075 --> 00:29:49.950 align:middle line:84%
So then you can train
your model on that data

00:29:49.950 --> 00:29:51.970 align:middle line:84%
and you can make the
model more robust to it.

00:29:51.970 --> 00:29:54.930 align:middle line:84%
So you end up with this
kind of adversarial play

00:29:54.930 --> 00:30:02.520 align:middle line:84%
similar to other defensive
operations, I assume.

00:30:02.520 --> 00:30:05.220 align:middle line:84%
But at any rate, that's
a bit of a tangent.

00:30:05.220 --> 00:30:07.245 align:middle line:84%
Any questions so
far on training?

00:30:07.245 --> 00:30:09.120 align:middle line:84%
We're going to do a dog
example really quick.

00:30:09.120 --> 00:30:14.660 align:middle line:90%


00:30:14.660 --> 00:30:16.640 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:30:16.640 --> 00:30:20.300 align:middle line:90%
So next example-- this thing.

00:30:20.300 --> 00:30:22.750 align:middle line:84%
So again, we're going to
assume the model gets it wrong.

00:30:22.750 --> 00:30:24.585 align:middle line:90%
So there's an update.

00:30:24.585 --> 00:30:25.960 align:middle line:84%
We're going to
describe this dog.

00:30:25.960 --> 00:30:26.470 align:middle line:90%
It's black.

00:30:26.470 --> 00:30:27.437 align:middle line:90%
It's got floppy ears.

00:30:27.437 --> 00:30:28.270 align:middle line:90%
It's got brown eyes.

00:30:28.270 --> 00:30:30.170 align:middle line:84%
It's got a pink
tongue, four legs.

00:30:30.170 --> 00:30:33.920 align:middle line:84%
Where's this going,
always or sometimes?

00:30:33.920 --> 00:30:34.670 align:middle line:90%
What do you think?

00:30:34.670 --> 00:30:37.220 align:middle line:90%


00:30:37.220 --> 00:30:37.720 align:middle line:90%
Always.

00:30:37.720 --> 00:30:39.620 align:middle line:84%
This is the only dog that
exists in the whole world.

00:30:39.620 --> 00:30:40.690 align:middle line:84%
This is what every
dog looks like.

00:30:40.690 --> 00:30:41.607 align:middle line:90%
Every dog is this dog.

00:30:41.607 --> 00:30:44.100 align:middle line:90%


00:30:44.100 --> 00:30:45.840 align:middle line:90%
Now, when we see--

00:30:45.840 --> 00:30:47.407 align:middle line:84%
oh, yeah, sorry,
dog, as we know.

00:30:47.407 --> 00:30:49.740 align:middle line:84%
Now, when we see this guy,
the model is going to update.

00:30:49.740 --> 00:30:51.720 align:middle line:84%
And now we have
that change, where

00:30:51.720 --> 00:30:54.000 align:middle line:84%
it's, OK, there's
two eyes still,

00:30:54.000 --> 00:30:55.530 align:middle line:90%
so every dog has two eyes.

00:30:55.530 --> 00:30:57.268 align:middle line:84%
Until we see a dog
with only one eye.

00:30:57.268 --> 00:30:58.810 align:middle line:84%
And now, the model
is like, OK, well,

00:30:58.810 --> 00:31:01.110 align:middle line:84%
it looks like most of the
time, dogs have two eyes,

00:31:01.110 --> 00:31:03.912 align:middle line:90%
and sometimes, they have one.

00:31:03.912 --> 00:31:06.000 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, statistical model.

00:31:06.000 --> 00:31:11.770 align:middle line:84%
But at this point, 100% of dogs
look exactly like this dog.

00:31:11.770 --> 00:31:14.020 align:middle line:90%
So that's how we train a model.

00:31:14.020 --> 00:31:18.190 align:middle line:84%
We now have a model of what
cats and dogs look like, or do,

00:31:18.190 --> 00:31:19.330 align:middle line:90%
or are.

00:31:19.330 --> 00:31:20.840 align:middle line:84%
So now, we go and
we test the model.

00:31:20.840 --> 00:31:22.540 align:middle line:84%
And it's really,
really critical,

00:31:22.540 --> 00:31:26.120 align:middle line:84%
when we test the
model, we use data

00:31:26.120 --> 00:31:28.240 align:middle line:84%
that the model has
not seen before.

00:31:28.240 --> 00:31:32.450 align:middle line:84%
If I give you the
exam in advance,

00:31:32.450 --> 00:31:35.040 align:middle line:84%
then you can just memorize
the questions and the answers.

00:31:35.040 --> 00:31:37.540 align:middle line:84%
That's not really helpful for
me to determine whether you've

00:31:37.540 --> 00:31:40.090 align:middle line:90%
actually learned the material.

00:31:40.090 --> 00:31:42.610 align:middle line:84%
I want to give you some sample
questions, sample answers,

00:31:42.610 --> 00:31:43.900 align:middle line:84%
and then on the test, I'll
give you different ones.

00:31:43.900 --> 00:31:45.400 align:middle line:84%
Because I want to
make sure that you

00:31:45.400 --> 00:31:47.110 align:middle line:84%
can generalize to
other problems,

00:31:47.110 --> 00:31:49.600 align:middle line:90%
to other kinds of questions.

00:31:49.600 --> 00:31:51.688 align:middle line:84%
If you just memorize
the solutions,

00:31:51.688 --> 00:31:53.230 align:middle line:84%
it's not helpful
for me to understand

00:31:53.230 --> 00:31:54.313 align:middle line:90%
whether you've learned it.

00:31:54.313 --> 00:31:55.690 align:middle line:84%
So when you're
testing the model,

00:31:55.690 --> 00:31:57.490 align:middle line:84%
it is absolutely
critical that you're

00:31:57.490 --> 00:31:59.860 align:middle line:84%
using data that the
model has not yet seen.

00:31:59.860 --> 00:32:01.870 align:middle line:84%
Now, in this case,
what we're finding is,

00:32:01.870 --> 00:32:03.115 align:middle line:90%
this thing is black.

00:32:03.115 --> 00:32:04.740 align:middle line:90%
It has two eyes.

00:32:04.740 --> 00:32:05.710 align:middle line:90%
Does have pointy ears.

00:32:05.710 --> 00:32:07.270 align:middle line:84%
But the thing is-- like,
and our model has never

00:32:07.270 --> 00:32:08.470 align:middle line:90%
seen a black cat before.

00:32:08.470 --> 00:32:10.690 align:middle line:90%
It's seen a black dog, though.

00:32:10.690 --> 00:32:12.470 align:middle line:90%
So now, our model is confused.

00:32:12.470 --> 00:32:12.970 align:middle line:90%
What is?

00:32:12.970 --> 00:32:17.442 align:middle line:90%


00:32:17.442 --> 00:32:18.400 align:middle line:90%
What am I referring to?

00:32:18.400 --> 00:32:19.817 align:middle line:84%
Anybody know what
this problem is?

00:32:19.817 --> 00:32:22.760 align:middle line:90%


00:32:22.760 --> 00:32:25.425 align:middle line:84%
SPEAKER 1: Answer
from participant:

00:32:25.425 --> 00:32:27.607 align:middle line:90%
SPEAKER 2: Boundary conditions?

00:32:27.607 --> 00:32:28.940 align:middle line:90%
BRANDON LESHCHINSKIY: Not quite.

00:32:28.940 --> 00:32:30.538 align:middle line:90%
This is a problem of bias.

00:32:30.538 --> 00:32:32.330 align:middle line:84%
This is a problem of
bias in your data set.

00:32:32.330 --> 00:32:35.290 align:middle line:90%
We don't have any black cats.

00:32:35.290 --> 00:32:38.540 align:middle line:84%
So our model is not great
at recognizing black cats.

00:32:38.540 --> 00:32:40.540 align:middle line:84%
Now, you can imagine,
in medical contexts,

00:32:40.540 --> 00:32:42.880 align:middle line:84%
this becomes really
problematic, because we're

00:32:42.880 --> 00:32:45.400 align:middle line:84%
training data on mostly
white people and mostly men,

00:32:45.400 --> 00:32:46.010 align:middle line:90%
for example.

00:32:46.010 --> 00:32:47.800 align:middle line:84%
And that doesn't help
for not-white people

00:32:47.800 --> 00:32:49.762 align:middle line:90%
and for not-men.

00:32:49.762 --> 00:32:51.720 align:middle line:84%
Because the model is
going to learn, as we saw,

00:32:51.720 --> 00:32:53.135 align:middle line:90%
statistics-- models are dumb.

00:32:53.135 --> 00:32:55.260 align:middle line:84%
Model's going to learn a
whole bunch of things that

00:32:55.260 --> 00:32:58.560 align:middle line:84%
are true about white people and
men, or about cats that are not

00:32:58.560 --> 00:33:02.800 align:middle line:84%
black, and then we're presented
with this other thing--

00:33:02.800 --> 00:33:04.722 align:middle line:90%
this other type of thing.

00:33:04.722 --> 00:33:06.430 align:middle line:84%
The model has no idea
what to do with it,

00:33:06.430 --> 00:33:08.320 align:middle line:84%
and just applies
the same assumptions

00:33:08.320 --> 00:33:10.200 align:middle line:90%
to this other thing.

00:33:10.200 --> 00:33:12.270 align:middle line:90%
So what's the lesson here?

00:33:12.270 --> 00:33:16.158 align:middle line:84%
Make sure that your training
data is representative.

00:33:16.158 --> 00:33:17.700 align:middle line:84%
And be very intentional
about the way

00:33:17.700 --> 00:33:19.783 align:middle line:84%
that you are collecting
training data to make sure

00:33:19.783 --> 00:33:22.570 align:middle line:90%
that it reflects the use case.

00:33:22.570 --> 00:33:27.060 align:middle line:84%
This goes back to
problem definition.

00:33:27.060 --> 00:33:29.980 align:middle line:84%
What problem are
we trying to solve?

00:33:29.980 --> 00:33:31.035 align:middle line:90%
Who is our end user?

00:33:31.035 --> 00:33:32.410 align:middle line:84%
What are we hoping
to accomplish?

00:33:32.410 --> 00:33:36.190 align:middle line:84%
All of these things that have
to happen in good engineering.

00:33:36.190 --> 00:33:37.630 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, this is a great example.

00:33:37.630 --> 00:33:39.713 align:middle line:84%
So if we use AI to define
what makes a good pilot,

00:33:39.713 --> 00:33:42.580 align:middle line:84%
the AI is going to say,
oh, probably white guys.

00:33:42.580 --> 00:33:43.705 align:middle line:90%
White guys are good pilots.

00:33:43.705 --> 00:33:49.193 align:middle line:90%


00:33:49.193 --> 00:33:50.860 align:middle line:84%
I can't solve this
problem for you guys,

00:33:50.860 --> 00:33:52.220 align:middle line:84%
but I can make you aware
of it so that you're

00:33:52.220 --> 00:33:53.345 align:middle line:90%
asking the right questions.

00:33:53.345 --> 00:33:56.483 align:middle line:90%


00:33:56.483 --> 00:33:58.150 align:middle line:84%
And of course, the
model will eventually

00:33:58.150 --> 00:34:01.240 align:middle line:84%
know that this is
a cat, hopefully.

00:34:01.240 --> 00:34:02.200 align:middle line:90%
So now, step seven.

00:34:02.200 --> 00:34:02.980 align:middle line:90%
Deploy the model.

00:34:02.980 --> 00:34:04.750 align:middle line:84%
You can do this on
Amazon Web Services.

00:34:04.750 --> 00:34:06.490 align:middle line:84%
You can make it
publicly accessible.

00:34:06.490 --> 00:34:09.940 align:middle line:84%
You can put it on a secure
hard drive somewhere.

00:34:09.940 --> 00:34:12.730 align:middle line:84%
However you guys
are going to use it,

00:34:12.730 --> 00:34:14.360 align:middle line:84%
that will dictate
how to deploy it.

00:34:14.360 --> 00:34:18.449 align:middle line:84%
So everything flows
from problem definition.

00:34:18.449 --> 00:34:20.460 align:middle line:90%
You can put it on a chip.

00:34:20.460 --> 00:34:25.370 align:middle line:84%
But to review, we
have, define a problem,

00:34:25.370 --> 00:34:30.860 align:middle line:84%
find data, clean data, choose
a model, train the model,

00:34:30.860 --> 00:34:32.659 align:middle line:90%
test the model--

00:34:32.659 --> 00:34:34.699 align:middle line:84%
whoopsies-- and then
deploy the model.

00:34:34.699 --> 00:34:37.417 align:middle line:90%


00:34:37.417 --> 00:34:38.500 align:middle line:90%
Questions on this process?

00:34:38.500 --> 00:34:42.159 align:middle line:90%


00:34:42.159 --> 00:34:44.927 align:middle line:84%
SPEAKER 1: Question
from participant:

00:34:44.927 --> 00:34:47.010 align:middle line:84%
SPEAKER 2: Is this the
typical approach regardless

00:34:47.010 --> 00:34:48.985 align:middle line:90%
of the model that is used?

00:34:48.985 --> 00:34:50.360 align:middle line:84%
BRANDON LESHCHINSKIY:
I think so.

00:34:50.360 --> 00:34:52.909 align:middle line:84%
Implicitly or explicitly,
this is generally the process.

00:34:52.909 --> 00:34:54.790 align:middle line:90%
Sorry, go ahead, Jennifer.

00:34:54.790 --> 00:34:57.595 align:middle line:84%
SPEAKER 2: Question
from participant:

00:34:57.595 --> 00:34:59.720 align:middle line:84%
SPEAKER 1: So if you showed
the model a guinea pig,

00:34:59.720 --> 00:35:02.300 align:middle line:84%
it would try to classify
it as a dog or cat

00:35:02.300 --> 00:35:05.320 align:middle line:84%
because those are the
options we had presented?

00:35:05.320 --> 00:35:06.570 align:middle line:90%
BRANDON LESHCHINSKIY: Correct.

00:35:06.570 --> 00:35:08.250 align:middle line:90%
So that's-- so that's step four.

00:35:08.250 --> 00:35:09.420 align:middle line:90%
What model are we using?

00:35:09.420 --> 00:35:12.840 align:middle line:84%
And the model architecture
might limit you.

00:35:12.840 --> 00:35:15.630 align:middle line:84%
There's only two outputs,
either cat or dog.

00:35:15.630 --> 00:35:18.600 align:middle line:84%
You don't have a third
output, you need a third--

00:35:18.600 --> 00:35:21.850 align:middle line:90%
you need to make space for that.

00:35:21.850 --> 00:35:24.537 align:middle line:84%
SPEAKER 1: Question
from participant:

00:35:24.537 --> 00:35:26.370 align:middle line:84%
SPEAKER 2: I'm working
on a master's degree,

00:35:26.370 --> 00:35:29.370 align:middle line:84%
and I completed a class
in data analytics.

00:35:29.370 --> 00:35:32.250 align:middle line:84%
I was working with R,
and one of the questions

00:35:32.250 --> 00:35:34.680 align:middle line:84%
I had at the end
of the class was,

00:35:34.680 --> 00:35:37.140 align:middle line:84%
is there an AI
program or progress

00:35:37.140 --> 00:35:39.245 align:middle line:90%
towards AI that clean data?

00:35:39.245 --> 00:35:40.620 align:middle line:84%
BRANDON LESHCHINSKIY:
There are--

00:35:40.620 --> 00:35:41.870 align:middle line:90%
I think they're working on it.

00:35:41.870 --> 00:35:44.970 align:middle line:84%
There's, like,
AutoML tools which

00:35:44.970 --> 00:35:48.780 align:middle line:84%
are trying to
democratize AI, where--

00:35:48.780 --> 00:35:51.930 align:middle line:84%
but even then, my
guess is that you

00:35:51.930 --> 00:35:55.750 align:middle line:84%
would have to input the data
in a way that it finds useful,

00:35:55.750 --> 00:35:57.810 align:middle line:90%
and then it takes it from there.

00:35:57.810 --> 00:35:59.910 align:middle line:84%
I don't know about
cleaning the data.

00:35:59.910 --> 00:36:02.530 align:middle line:90%
I really don't know.

00:36:02.530 --> 00:36:03.970 align:middle line:84%
Any other questions
before we move

00:36:03.970 --> 00:36:05.907 align:middle line:90%
on to summary and conclusion?

00:36:05.907 --> 00:36:13.870 align:middle line:90%


00:36:13.870 --> 00:36:14.650 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:36:14.650 --> 00:36:18.055 align:middle line:84%
So to conclude, we've
covered an intro

00:36:18.055 --> 00:36:19.180 align:middle line:90%
to artificial intelligence.

00:36:19.180 --> 00:36:21.430 align:middle line:84%
We've seen, step by step,
how to train an algorithm.

00:36:21.430 --> 00:36:23.530 align:middle line:84%
And to close, I
want to leave you

00:36:23.530 --> 00:36:26.560 align:middle line:84%
all with four questions
and a word of caution.

00:36:26.560 --> 00:36:29.740 align:middle line:84%
Four questions you
should ask about any AI

00:36:29.740 --> 00:36:32.540 align:middle line:84%
that you encounter,
whether you're

00:36:32.540 --> 00:36:35.018 align:middle line:90%
trying to assess it or use it.

00:36:35.018 --> 00:36:36.560 align:middle line:84%
Four questions you
should always ask.

00:36:36.560 --> 00:36:37.970 align:middle line:90%
Number one: What is the goal?

00:36:37.970 --> 00:36:39.080 align:middle line:90%
Problem definition, right?

00:36:39.080 --> 00:36:40.482 align:middle line:90%
What is the goal?

00:36:40.482 --> 00:36:41.690 align:middle line:90%
What problem are you solving?

00:36:41.690 --> 00:36:43.760 align:middle line:84%
What are its inputs
and its outputs?

00:36:43.760 --> 00:36:45.820 align:middle line:90%
What does the thing do?

00:36:45.820 --> 00:36:46.570 align:middle line:90%
That's number one.

00:36:46.570 --> 00:36:49.552 align:middle line:84%
Number two: What is
the training data?

00:36:49.552 --> 00:36:50.510 align:middle line:90%
Where did it come from?

00:36:50.510 --> 00:36:51.418 align:middle line:90%
How was it prepared?

00:36:51.418 --> 00:36:52.960 align:middle line:84%
How did you make
sure-- did you check

00:36:52.960 --> 00:36:54.520 align:middle line:90%
for this bias or that bias?

00:36:54.520 --> 00:36:56.228 align:middle line:84%
How did you make sure
that it's balanced?

00:36:56.228 --> 00:36:59.820 align:middle line:90%


00:36:59.820 --> 00:37:01.770 align:middle line:84%
Number three: What kind
of model did you use?

00:37:01.770 --> 00:37:02.760 align:middle line:90%
Why?

00:37:02.760 --> 00:37:04.500 align:middle line:90%
Is it interpretable?

00:37:04.500 --> 00:37:06.243 align:middle line:90%
Should it be?

00:37:06.243 --> 00:37:07.410 align:middle line:90%
How did you pick this model?

00:37:07.410 --> 00:37:10.492 align:middle line:90%


00:37:10.492 --> 00:37:12.700 align:middle line:84%
And number four: What's the
accuracy when you test it

00:37:12.700 --> 00:37:15.100 align:middle line:84%
on new data, when you
test it on testing data--

00:37:15.100 --> 00:37:16.357 align:middle line:90%
not training data.

00:37:16.357 --> 00:37:18.940 align:middle line:84%
When you give it a whole new set
of data that it's never seen,

00:37:18.940 --> 00:37:20.440 align:middle line:90%
what is its accuracy?

00:37:20.440 --> 00:37:23.193 align:middle line:90%


00:37:23.193 --> 00:37:25.360 align:middle line:84%
The reinforcement learning
version of this would be,

00:37:25.360 --> 00:37:27.670 align:middle line:84%
what happens when you test
it in a different simulation

00:37:27.670 --> 00:37:29.230 align:middle line:90%
or in the real world?

00:37:29.230 --> 00:37:31.420 align:middle line:84%
But something-- there has
to be some question about,

00:37:31.420 --> 00:37:33.160 align:middle line:84%
how well does this
actually do when

00:37:33.160 --> 00:37:35.830 align:middle line:84%
it's given a problem
that it has not had

00:37:35.830 --> 00:37:37.960 align:middle line:90%
the opportunity to memorize?

00:37:37.960 --> 00:37:41.092 align:middle line:84%
So goal, training
data, model, accuracy.

00:37:41.092 --> 00:37:42.800 align:middle line:84%
If you keep these four
questions in mind,

00:37:42.800 --> 00:37:48.350 align:middle line:84%
you'll be better able to assess
any AI system you encounter.

00:37:48.350 --> 00:37:51.970 align:middle line:84%
And now, let me close
with a word of caution.

00:37:51.970 --> 00:37:55.520 align:middle line:84%
Developing an AI model
can be very difficult,

00:37:55.520 --> 00:37:59.970 align:middle line:84%
can also be very valuable, and
it can also be very seductive.

00:37:59.970 --> 00:38:02.700 align:middle line:90%
It is a task.

00:38:02.700 --> 00:38:06.060 align:middle line:84%
If the task does not belong
to a strategy in support

00:38:06.060 --> 00:38:09.420 align:middle line:84%
of certain objectives that
measure a mission that reflect

00:38:09.420 --> 00:38:12.300 align:middle line:84%
values, it is not
a good use of time,

00:38:12.300 --> 00:38:13.470 align:middle line:90%
even if it is really cool.

00:38:13.470 --> 00:38:17.360 align:middle line:90%


00:38:17.360 --> 00:38:23.163 align:middle line:84%
So when I say goal, I'm talking,
again, both at a low level--

00:38:23.163 --> 00:38:24.580 align:middle line:84%
inputs and outputs
of this model--

00:38:24.580 --> 00:38:25.780 align:middle line:90%
but also at a high level.

00:38:25.780 --> 00:38:27.100 align:middle line:90%
What are we trying to do?

00:38:27.100 --> 00:38:28.930 align:middle line:84%
And it's important
that we constantly

00:38:28.930 --> 00:38:31.630 align:middle line:84%
reevaluate the low-level
goals with respect

00:38:31.630 --> 00:38:33.580 align:middle line:90%
to the high-level problem.

00:38:33.580 --> 00:38:35.560 align:middle line:84%
We want to situate the
model, and the training,

00:38:35.560 --> 00:38:42.390 align:middle line:84%
and the data within a broader
problem-solving context.

00:38:42.390 --> 00:38:45.360 align:middle line:84%
If I were looking
at the Air Force

00:38:45.360 --> 00:38:47.910 align:middle line:84%
and trying to figure
out, where would AI--

00:38:47.910 --> 00:38:51.540 align:middle line:84%
or where to implement
AI, I would start here.

00:38:51.540 --> 00:38:54.540 align:middle line:84%
I would start with identifying,
what are the values?

00:38:54.540 --> 00:38:58.260 align:middle line:84%
What's the mission,
objectives, strategy, tasks?

00:38:58.260 --> 00:39:00.800 align:middle line:90%
Kind of as a tree.

00:39:00.800 --> 00:39:02.270 align:middle line:90%
And I would identify problems.

00:39:02.270 --> 00:39:06.270 align:middle line:84%
What are the gaps between our
aspirations and our reality?

00:39:06.270 --> 00:39:08.040 align:middle line:84%
Then I would think
critically about

00:39:08.040 --> 00:39:09.960 align:middle line:84%
which gaps artificial
intelligence actually

00:39:09.960 --> 00:39:13.050 align:middle line:84%
could fill and whether
those gaps would be

00:39:13.050 --> 00:39:16.480 align:middle line:90%
partially or completely filled.

00:39:16.480 --> 00:39:19.382 align:middle line:84%
If you have data that's unused,
that presents an opportunity.

00:39:19.382 --> 00:39:21.340 align:middle line:84%
If you have tasks that
require a lot of people,

00:39:21.340 --> 00:39:22.510 align:middle line:90%
that presents an opportunity.

00:39:22.510 --> 00:39:24.385 align:middle line:84%
If you have tasks that
are complex or require

00:39:24.385 --> 00:39:26.610 align:middle line:84%
a lot of thinking
and processing data,

00:39:26.610 --> 00:39:28.410 align:middle line:84%
those are problems
where AI can help.

00:39:28.410 --> 00:39:31.000 align:middle line:90%


00:39:31.000 --> 00:39:34.120 align:middle line:84%
If you have a problem with drug
overdoses or with suicides,

00:39:34.120 --> 00:39:36.640 align:middle line:84%
AI is probably
not going to help.

00:39:36.640 --> 00:39:42.143 align:middle line:84%
And in fact, I've heard of
cases where people collect data,

00:39:42.143 --> 00:39:44.060 align:middle line:84%
and they do all of this
quantitative analysis,

00:39:44.060 --> 00:39:48.130 align:middle line:84%
but this is fundamentally
a human problem.

00:39:48.130 --> 00:39:50.310 align:middle line:84%
And so it can be actually
quite distracting.

00:39:50.310 --> 00:39:52.705 align:middle line:84%
So my point is simply
that, at its best,

00:39:52.705 --> 00:39:55.080 align:middle line:84%
AI can make the Air Force more
efficient, more effective.

00:39:55.080 --> 00:39:57.570 align:middle line:84%
At its worst, it can
distract us from actually

00:39:57.570 --> 00:40:03.442 align:middle line:84%
solving difficult, challenging
human and cultural problems.

00:40:03.442 --> 00:40:05.150 align:middle line:84%
So it's really important
to be discerning

00:40:05.150 --> 00:40:06.590 align:middle line:84%
about what kind of problem
you're trying to solve

00:40:06.590 --> 00:40:07.790 align:middle line:90%
and where AI can help.

00:40:07.790 --> 00:40:09.560 align:middle line:84%
But at this point,
I hope, and I think

00:40:09.560 --> 00:40:11.852 align:middle line:84%
that you have the tools to
prevent that from happening,

00:40:11.852 --> 00:40:14.270 align:middle line:84%
and to harness this very
powerful new technology

00:40:14.270 --> 00:40:17.000 align:middle line:84%
in support of building a
more effective Air Force

00:40:17.000 --> 00:40:18.720 align:middle line:84%
and pursuing
America's interests.

00:40:18.720 --> 00:40:20.690 align:middle line:90%
So thank you all so much.

00:40:20.690 --> 00:40:22.700 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to stick
around for questions,

00:40:22.700 --> 00:40:26.500 align:middle line:84%
and I will leave you with
this conclusion slide.

00:40:26.500 --> 00:40:40.000 align:middle line:90%